BUG: nears-qa.md still describes the reclaim as "rm -rf + your own mkdir", which the
     guard deliberately does NOT do
File: .claude/agents/nears-qa.md line 47 (section "Device pool — acquire before booting")
Severity: low | breaks_ac: false (reasoning below) | surface: docs (shipped by this change)

Doc text (line 47, "Stale locks" bullet):

  - **Stale locks:** `qa_lock_acquire` reclaims them for you -- a lock whose pid is dead,
    or whose `anchor_started` no longer matches the live pid (pid reuse), is stale; note
    the reclaim in the envelope. **The disk precheck above does NOT gate this step** --
    reclaiming a dead-pid lock is just `rm -rf` + your own `mkdir`, it needs no additional
    disk, ...
                                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

The code under test explicitly rejects rm -rf as the reclaim primitive:

  scripts/qa-lock-guard.sh:356-369 (comment in qa_lock_acquire)
    # The reclaim is a compare-and-swap on a shared path, and `rm -rf` cannot be
    # its primitive: `rm -rf` removes whatever is at the path, so two racers
    # could each delete the OTHER's already-valid lock and both come away
    # believing they hold the device -- precisely the cross-session clobber this
    # ticket exists to close.
    # `mv` (rename(2)) IS atomic, so it becomes the claim token: ...

  scripts/qa-lock-guard.sh:398-408 (the actual mechanism)
    if ! holder="$(_qa_lock_reserve_reclaim "$locks_dir" "$serial")"; then ... fi
    reclaimed="$holder/lock"
    if ! mv "$lock_dir" "$reclaimed" 2>/dev/null; then ... fi

i.e. reserve-by-mkdir + atomic mv + re-validate + only then rm the moved-aside copy.

Why I judged breaks_ac: false rather than failing AC4 ("no drift between doc and code"):
the clause is vestigial from the pre-NEARS-1689 bullet, when the QA agent performed the
reclaim by hand. Its operative claim is about DISK COST ("it needs no additional disk"),
which remains true, and the same bullet's instruction now correctly delegates the whole
step to `qa_lock_acquire`. No reader following this section would perform an rm -rf: the
section's own rule two bullets earlier is "Acquire and release with the code, never by
hand". Every scheme element an agent actually acts on (API, owner.json fields, anchor
semantics, reclaim triggers, release refusal, same-session warning, 800MB floor) was
verified matching the code. Flagging it so the team lead can overrule this judgement if
they read AC4 more strictly.

Suggested fix: drop the mechanism words -- "reclaiming a dead-pid lock needs no
additional disk" carries the entire argument.

